\DOC X86_XACCSTEP_TAC

\TYPE {X86_XACCSTEP_TAC : thm * (thm option array) -> term list -> bool -> string -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Symbolically executes one x86 instruction of the program in an
{eventually}-style goal, advancing the goal's state variable to a fresh
successor state and re-deriving all state-component assumptions for it.

\DESCRIBE
The arguments are {th : thm * (thm option array)}, the program's code-length
theorem paired with a cache of per-instruction decoding theorems, used to step
the loaded bytes; {excs : term list}, terms serving as exceptions/extra data
for the step; {aflag : bool}, a flag selecting how the step is performed (in
the observed traces it is computed per instruction from the step number); and
{s : string}, the name given to the state after the step. The conclusion is
rewritten with the new state name, the postcondition {P} being unchanged. All
assumptions mentioning the old state are discarded and replaced by
corresponding assumptions about the new state: every surviving register, flag
and memory read, the {bytes_loaded} assumption, and the accumulated {MAYCHANGE}
frame (sometimes extended with further components written by the instruction).
The instruction's effect appears as new reads of the updated components, an
updated program-counter assumption, and updated flag assumptions; arithmetic
facts about the freshly introduced result variables (and any flag-defining
conditions) are added, typically migrating into the block of purely arithmetic
assumptions kept at the front of the assumption list.
{
               A ?- eventually x86 P t
     ==================================================  X86_XACCSTEP_TAC th excs aflag s
               A' ?- eventually x86 P u
}
\noindent Here {u} is the new state term and {A'} is {A} with all old-state
assumptions replaced by their new-state counterparts plus the new facts, among
which may be zero or more new arithmetic equations.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single-step, one-subgoal behaviour, always
on goals of this {eventually} form with large assumption sets about machine
state.

\ENDDOC
